2. Types of Blockchains
While your lab manual focuses heavily on the features of blockchain, a complete survey requires defining the four primary architectural types:

Public Blockchains: Completely open, decentralized networks where anyone can join, read, write, and participate in the consensus process (e.g., Bitcoin, Ethereum).

Private Blockchains: Permissioned networks controlled by a single organization. Only authorized users can participate, making them faster but centralized.

Consortium (Federated) Blockchains: Governed by a group of organizations rather than a single entity. It offers a hybrid of decentralization and controlled access, ideal for banks or allied businesses.

Hybrid Blockchains: Combines public and private elements, allowing organizations to keep certain data private while verifying specific transactions on a public ledger.

